2186308276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "db530145-a259-5aa0-aa5d-dbfcaff7cc5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-334021" :question/problem "Given the set of functions ${\\,7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ defined on $x\\in R,$ select the correct options in the following reasoning.For all $x\\in R,$  we have that\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\cdot 7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,+\\,{{field-3}}\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince \n{{field-4}} \nthe scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly \n{{field-5}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the set of functions ${{f}_{1}(x),{f}_{2}(x),…,{f}_{n}(x)},$ which are defined on some interval $I,$ is *linearly dependent* if there exist real scalars ${k}_{1},{k}_{2},…,{k}_{n}$ not all zero such that the linear combination ${k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}$ is identically zero, i.e, \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}(x)+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}(x)+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}(x)=0\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in I.$ Otherwise, if ${k}_{1}={k}_{2}=⋯={k}_{n}=0$ is the only solution, the set is *linearly independent*.\n\nNow, for all $x\\in R,$ we have that \n\n$$\n0\\cdot 7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+\\,4\\cdot 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,+\\,4\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\text{not all}$ the scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly $\\text{dependent}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308559 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "225a1eff-527f-50a3-a950-0e07356c0c8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305479} {:answer/id #uuid "88e4ac3e-acf7-5f50-9fef-7c0d6c5127de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306460}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306460} :answer-field/id #uuid "164c75dd-1d7b-5483-8626-94f363941660" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307590} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4375f462-ef6a-5d9e-bf5b-d24ce61a1925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305816} {:answer/id #uuid "6180adb3-8d26-5a19-9ce0-e694e204c090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306091} {:answer/id #uuid "9f97eab8-1e38-5b7d-963e-d85bc898a662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306647} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e467cf-6110-5dc9-a27d-22ab45048e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307082}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305816} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e38717e-3ffb-5a34-9c57-bc6ca0a2c3bb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307970} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "129f96e0-7d7f-589c-a331-e0eb85b217cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305314} {:answer/id #uuid "798b8103-db44-5e23-8007-6cac7e843eb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306308}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306308} :answer-field/id #uuid "b17a5a7a-6c0f-508a-b7cf-5291f1a8c8cc" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5a272db4-485b-515e-b75d-0755cc7e94a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-334109" :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}+1,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=2+2{x}^{2},\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  which is \n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{x}^{2}+1 & 2+2{x}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+1)^{′} & (2+2{x}^{2})^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{x}^{2}+1 & 2+2{x}^{2} \\\\ 2x & 4x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)(4x)-(2+2{x}^{2})(2x) \\\\  & =4x({x}^{2}+1)-4x({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308561 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16a800c5-7e18-59ee-94e7-80cdfd4de19a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305356} {:answer/id #uuid "a39ab598-0b8d-5c62-ad42-23748d01375c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306694}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305356} :answer-field/id #uuid "41b09b08-7233-5ed9-8300-197e20e68046" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307724} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a221672-bcdc-5f01-bcdf-22405659444f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306315} {:answer/id #uuid "896dce62-f5b3-52c1-8dd8-bc6d2e4c1097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306465} {:answer/id #uuid "a265e633-dc0a-5983-8255-9b4e361560b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306677} {:answer/id #uuid "d1a73cc5-5dc5-51a3-9906-8c4d81c74c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307095}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307095} :answer-field/id #uuid "9248dce7-b98e-5ff4-bda2-e01c8b11790b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307983} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10fea060-278f-50a3-9b56-183bdf163c45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305306} {:answer/id #uuid "d556e851-090a-5509-bf2f-41c8241ee8df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307130}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307130} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2c59f88-c92c-51fe-84a6-89edef0e2468" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b67df1a9-5674-599d-941a-796b1c85ba70" :question/math-academy-id "q-334118" :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=x+1,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2},\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}x+1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ (x+1)^{′} & ({x}^{2})^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}x+1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(x+1)\\cdot (2x)-{x}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2x(x+1)-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+2x-{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+2x \\\\  & =x(x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308562 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56a767ea-664e-50a2-a2a9-ec9361cfa274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305990} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8826e3-2ec4-5554-86d5-cc7e46b0422c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306501}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306501} :answer-field/id #uuid "94aa5ac7-2d4a-5d7f-9c4e-6fb13a1afc3e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #e