w.\n\n$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}x & {x}^{3}+1 \\\\ (x)^{′} & ({x}^{3}+1)^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}x & {x}^{3}+1 \\\\ 1 & 3{x}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =x\\cdot (3{x}^{2})-({x}^{3}+1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}-{x}^{3}-1 \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308563 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d45f5cb-a6c3-5e02-88ea-67413e9ac9ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305267} {:answer/id #uuid "4d9afda2-c0eb-508f-b0b6-0915d5a95e07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305904} {:answer/id #uuid "8e51d8ad-625d-5e7d-9f00-319074ea1b4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306500} {:answer/id #uuid "c90f0b5e-6eb8-5341-a120-b4321657b279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307023}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307023} :answer-field/id #uuid "85f1f4d5-eb8e-5e66-8437-d61f873025d9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307938} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9644b0c6-6f52-5be7-9e9c-bd943465155a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306565} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc77034-2c85-5f03-b8d3-1ebc7f08a5be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306650} {:answer/id #uuid "f3f783bb-7039-5070-9263-0991d3c26bd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307410}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306650} :answer-field/id #uuid "9270ac1d-3361-53be-aa59-717b7c3c8a89" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307984} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "499870aa-5bd6-5723-92d9-82d9a31dd1ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305867} {:answer/id #uuid "551470fe-04f5-5a40-8ec4-1264320e5507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305975} {:answer/id #uuid "ae29676d-86eb-50c5-9d33-a95a3051fe16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306796}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305867} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a9729fb-0da9-5933-8089-514f7f716235" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7d233510-7420-5376-8eba-7542190a5932" :question/math-academy-id "q-334121" :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x},\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{x} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ ({e}^{x})^{′} & ({e}^{2x})^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{x} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ {e}^{x} & 2{e}^{2x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & =2{e}^{3x}-{e}^{3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308575 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4bfddbc-eab6-5c68-8480-bef738a56270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466151} {:answer/id #uuid "64147964-eec7-52c1-9f57-1c149a73f852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466152}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466151} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f74ff9e-c2b0-5458-991c-e454a3497dc8" :answer-field/key "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466919} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dfa3625-eb85-5c68-87b3-150c09ab9de2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466153} {:answer/id #uuid "2a1fbef8-4d8a-5576-b0b9-bdc5b60be018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466154}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466154} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4c8e58a-6367-584c-8e40-5f4fdf246ca5" :answer-field/key "${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466920} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cc80953-1aab-5c6b-9404-d800b2762c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466155} {:answer/id #uuid "b9a54cc8-2891-5cca-bcb5-3ffe47599973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466156}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466155} :answer-field/id #uuid "5faeb251-e31b-580b-bd7e-425b6f76dd02" :answer-field/key "The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466921} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cd685e6-acc5-5300-8f25-0e60dc515d95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668539} {:answer/id #uuid "29f13f51-d012-500f-a7de-4f350b16e012" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668540}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186668539} :answer-field/id #uuid "72d5b9cb-fe87-5d34-870a-5cf59bad280a" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d23e4c59-133c-5f47-8ffc-1426beb350c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-334199" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation” is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{e}^{3x}-3(3{e}^{3x})+2({e}^{3x}) & =0 \\\\ 8 & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation” is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2x}-3(2{e}^{2x})+2({e}^{2x}) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation” is false. Since ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308586 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcf9011c-88d2-522f-b3b2-7f5e58324603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466165} {:answer/id #uuid "7c54ae2f-cbec-5b96-bc9f-83b395505baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466166}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466165} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b76786e-84b5-5680-8436-15f590623f91" :answer-field/key "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466926} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e52cab96-26d7-57ef-a7bd-1454284967ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466167} {:answer/id #uuid "08e922da-3532-5a0c-84aa-77682d68a282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466168}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466168} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1740d26-3686-55da-8705-422b2c2b00b7" :answer-field/key "${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466927} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b152814-ca69-5689-a07f-ea7a68ee13fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466169} {:answer/id #uuid "800df2b3-aa45-578a-a674-f96d56bef770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466170}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466169} :answer-field/id #uuid "243b5076-4595-54da-af25-5a1ce4f33b19" :answer-field/key "The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466928} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "058b97d2-5b6c-5fd2-85ec-1ec9c8532ee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668543} {:answer/id #uuid "79b2c63a-c96a-5408-80c6-47f37d11ee73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668544}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186668543} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9825095-3b77-52eb-b94a-81f7a8fc958a" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b4674804-88cb-5499-be8a-d4a228b08c3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-334258" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+5y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation” is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{2x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-2{e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+5{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ 2(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x){e}^{2x} & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation” is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))+5{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation” is false. Since ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308587 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e465ed0-28fa-5b9f-95ed-79a40660d4a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466171} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf2c758-ce80-5412-81b1-ca972c5051a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466172}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466172} :answer-field/id #uuid "88051d80-7e0f-5259-9d11-0f02843706d0" :answer-field/key "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466929} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01021e8a-2f30-5b1d-a446-fda33121cb10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466173} {:answer/id #uuid "50966b7e-00d2-5249-aaf0-4c04b384797c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466174}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466174} :answer-field/id #uuid "b80788d5-ebcc-59ed-92b0-24cc521fff81" :answer-field/key "${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x-1}$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466930} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d21b577-109d-5642-8668-cb6bc9643365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466175} {:answer/id #uuid "440e17f7-9b90-5761-8be1-b41c114d1c4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466176}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466175} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b49c89f-2bd2-546f-b4fc-5889ac48750d" :answer-field/key "The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466931} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac59cf60-4567-5ac0-815d-6cbb3c7a55c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668545} {:answer/id #uuid "4f17f9c5-a3c8-509b-ae3b-b23b2a3b1b0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668546}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186668545} :answer-field/id #uuid "d255ab09-9825-53e0-957b-b6ca841b72bc" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ce7557df-4bdb-5791-b8df-2efda5a01a24" :question/math-academy-id "q-334260" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}-6{y}^{′}+9y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{e}^{3x}-6\\cdot 3{e}^{3x}+9{e}^{3x} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x-1}$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{3x-1} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{3x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{e}^{3x-1}-6\\cdot 3{e}^{3x-1}+9{e}^{3x-1} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation” is false. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{3x} & {e}^{3x-1} \\\\ 3{e}^{3x} & 3{e}^{3x-1}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\cdot 3{e}^{3x-1}-3{e}^{3x}\\cdot {e}^{3x-1} \\\\  & =3{e}^{6x-1}-3{e}^{6x-1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0$ for all $x\\in R,$ the functions are not linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation”; “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x-1}$ is a solution to the equation” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308589 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b216db04-2d87-5f29-b031-4b8f18aebbe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466177} {:answer/id #uuid "084e80ea-fab8-56b6-840f-252ff612f881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466178}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466178} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d31f03f-7baf-56e7-b1ac-efd300ef99ba" :answer-field/key "${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466932} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff9dc928-9b30-5b8d-8c18-90d8a6534ab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466179} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb19be9-60e0-5255-a502-12dbdfe06395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466180}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466180} :answer-field/id #uuid "20d0b221-8cd2-5d31-84e5-81ec105281e0" :answer-field/key "${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466933} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22e26dd4-c78f-596b-9c14-f45264b02075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466181} {:answer/id #uuid "c433f3b3-d4f1-587f-a97d-e0085ce49482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466182}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466182} :answer-field/id #uuid "98ee50d6-62fb-5851-9ee5-5ce5a71ca168" :answer-field/key "The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466934} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9be53f63-80c4-5335-8318-ed7055a70224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668547} {:answer/id #uuid "9964a441-16f0-5da0-b569-673b8fc47c0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668548}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186668547} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e625453-8442-5df6-b6cd-303220d221cf" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fbddaa01-5158-5d3b-b619-76268088aef1" :question/math-academy-id "q-334266" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-3x{y}^{′}+4y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =2x \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot 2-3x\\cdot 2x+4{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-6{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3)-3x(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x)+4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3{x}^{2}-6{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-3{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$” is true. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{x}^{2} & {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\ 2x & 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x)-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+{x}^{3}-2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not identically $0$ on $(0,\\infty ),$ the functions are linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation”; “${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$”; “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$”."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308590 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff45dd62-df17-5749-8a41-f08eebe6dbbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466183} {:answer/id #uuid "7d206c86-5689-5b2c-b54e-7232892b3bc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466184}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466184} :answer-field/id #uuid "9891fc66-c238-53f0-a9cc-3f49c6b8b39a" :answer-field/key "${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466935} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db3e1dcf-3b31-518b-8484-c581cf7c83d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466185} {:answer/id #uuid "c434960a-d94c-5537-a03f-4ed019706c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466186}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466186} :answer-field/id #uuid "29753554-0e6b-5e44-9b7f-3c704824a70e" :answer-field/key "${y}_{2}(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466936} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6eb2349-e66a-55f0-ab55-a4d8940ad484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466187} {:answer/id #uuid "29697802-a7e8-5fcd-be1a-e444edb2e8bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466188}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466187} :answer-field/id #uuid "326f1276-eef3-5446-91bb-9631e7289f2c" :answer-field/key "The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466937} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "243a34a3-876e-5e9a-b44f-428820b9dc61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668549} {:answer/id #uuid "1c4dcd46-fde6-5021-a456-f43166f6de2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668550}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186668549} :answer-field/id #uuid "06cfd4ff-1bb4-5910-ad1f-556743ac63cb" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3a08136e-69ff-52a7-8d77-c9b9c2b3571c" :question/math-academy-id "q-334278" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation” is false. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\cdot (-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0$ for all $x\\in R,$ the functions are not linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation”; “${y}_{2}(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308738 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54f141e1-5d1e-5129-8a5d-1ce524c3a538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=-k(150-N)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305973} {:answer/id #uuid "566d884b-c307-5cb5-900e-d4e916f6e60a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=k{N}^{2}(150-N)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305986} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ccb96d-8146-58e1-a658-cc117cb30fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=-k(150+N)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306742} {:answer/id #uuid "ead33e72-291a-5706-b666-4290765ed58c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=k(150-N)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307313} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e7bf3e-f305-5b82-8c10-c924a8fa96ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=kN(150-N)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307453}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307313} :answer-field/id #uuid "fea18a22-4aec-53d2-b22b-ca7ff8019a1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "54bac72f-651e-55b5-8b4b-fbc4e753ebc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-335135" :question/problem "A wildlife manager noted that the growth of the number of deer in a protected forest is directly proportional to the difference between the forest’s maximum deer population and the current population. Let $N(t)$ be the deer population, measured in number of deer, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum deer population that the forest can support is $150$ deer, which differential equation could be used to model the forest’s deer population? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the deer population $N(t)$ of the forest changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum deer population the forest can support and the current deer population. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-N),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum deer population the forest can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=150\\text{ deer},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=k(150-N).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308739 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04aedd39-6cd0-5b6c-a95b-8f1528463bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(95-T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305194} {:answer/id #uuid "100e6d8a-679a-59a7-9f49-904c4ba960dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(30-T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305298} {:answer/id #uuid "3d8045ca-7194-5335-8e8c-fb093e38eb2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(60-T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305744} {:answer/id #uuid "7c223239-ad05-5364-8640-ceef9053e8b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(100-T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306336} {:answer/id #uuid "eb832421-670f-5c24-a2cd-1bebc2d73fe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(950-T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307318}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305194} :answer-field/id #uuid "69e66b97-0745-5e34-a590-bf57a3e5e855" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5651275c-fd64-5799-85e0-52ab15ebd288" :question/math-academy-id "q-335142" :question/problem "A tutoring center currently has $10$ enrolled students. The number of students in the center $T(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in months. If the maximum capacity of the tutoring center is $95$ students, which differential equation could be used to model the student population of the center? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Since the number of students in the center obeys an inhibited growth model, the rate at which the number of students changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the number of students in the center. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-T),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the tutoring center, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=95$ students, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k(95-T).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308740 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0be596d2-348f-52d1-bdbb-a8f9262ef683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dM}{dt}=k(35-M)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305252} {:answer/id #uuid "1cc9a611-32b3-5c07-adf4-0f5db91760e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dM}{dt}=k(60-M)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305416} {:answer/id #uuid "1fbeaf46-358d-53c4-a292-7cfd16a6e157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dM}{dt}=k(150-M)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305460} {:answer/id #uuid "2b90d980-e2c2-5e82-8176-18a5784c6485" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dM}{dt}=k(250-M)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305569} {:answer/id #uuid "6a37b76e-f780-59e2-9d76-b4af35790d12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dM}{dt}=k(50-M)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306166}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305416} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7bbd6e9-b260-5c66-af8a-149b7483e375" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5e2af3a7-6f0f-5f5a-a5bb-60da7750b37e" :question/math-academy-id "q-335145" :question/problem "A marine rehabilitation aquarium initially houses $25$ marine animals. The number of animals in the aquarium $M(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in months. If the maximum capacity of the aquarium is $60$ animals, which differential equation could be used to model the animal population? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Since the number of animals in the aquarium obeys an inhibited growth model, the rate at which the number of animals changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the number of animals in the aquarium. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-M),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the aquarium, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=60$ animals, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=k(60-M).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311566 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0802b9a4-f869-51aa-9ad2-f5c4ec4ede2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$19$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308807} {:answer/id #uuid "79af7521-db1a-5890-848a-9723cc01378d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$14$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309697} {:answer/id #uuid "851d5afb-e8fd-5832-aa19-7aad8ee508b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$12$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309784} {:answer/id #uuid "cc811dc3-bbd1-58f9-aae7-865a2b20e03b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$10$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310387} {:answer/id #uuid "d7357cc4-db50-5841-9068-42178a678657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$8$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310482}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309697} :answer-field/id #uuid "099de7c6-8163-5944-a855-904c946df6a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9ba6467f-d5e7-52b0-bc6e-1edae87450cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-335280" :question/problem "A can of soda is taken out of a refrigerator and left in a room that stays at a constant temperature of $20{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ The soda’s temperature $T(t)$ (in degrees Celsius) can be modeled by the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(20-T),\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\ge 0$ is the time, measured in minutes, and $k>0$ is a constant. The initial temperature of the soda is $8{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and after $5$ minutes, the soda has warmed to $12{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the soda to reach $16{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}?$" :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the can is modeled by the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(20-T).\n$$\n\nWe separate variables and integrate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{20-T} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{20-T} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|20-T| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $20-T>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20-T & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 20-T & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 20-T & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =20-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the initial temperature was $8{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=8,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =20-K{e}^{0} \\\\ 8 & =20-K \\\\ K & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=20-12{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $5$ minutes, the temperature of the soda was $12{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ So, applying the condition $T(5)=12,$  we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =20-12{e}^{-5k} \\\\ 8 & =12{e}^{-5k} \\\\ {e}^{-5k} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -5k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$  be the time when the soda can reaches $16{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =16 \\\\ 20-12{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =16 \\\\ 4 & =12{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the value of $k,$ we get:\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}{-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}=13.547…≈14\\text{ }\\text{minutes},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311567 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "156ded23-ce88-53d3-a639-9d820eb09a01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1$ minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308909} {:answer/id #uuid "8a644610-216c-5c70-96be-6978f05c7dd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$3$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309835} {:answer/id #uuid "9a4012d6-aa43-57f5-b933-67f5ae4bf20a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309990} {:answer/id #uuid "c0fcc147-8682-560f-8b96-51d7e1858066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310305} {:answer/id #uuid "f721e0cb-70f5-51e1-acaf-e8fb8aacbddd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$5$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310714}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308909} :answer-field/id #uuid "47f6fe49-3d76-5e25-8d26-c4ee0c65a685" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "645d4a44-d927-5a86-b205-e23eb598edfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-335282" :question/problem "The volume $X(t)$ (in $\\text{L}$) of water in a container as it fills toward its maximum capacity can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dX}{dt}=0.4(3-X),\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\ge 0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial volume is $1.5\\,\\text{L},$ how long does it take for the container to reach a volume of $2\\,\\text{L}?$ Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the container's volume is modeled by the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dX}{dt}=0.4(3-X).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dX}{3-X} & =0.4\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dX}{3-X} & =\\int 0.4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-X| & =0.4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $3-X>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $X(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-X & ={e}^{-0.4t-C} \\\\ 3-X & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-0.4t} \\\\ 3-X & =K{e}^{-0.4t} \\\\ X & =3-K{e}^{-0.4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the initial volume was $1.5\\,\\text{L}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $X(0)=1.5,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.5 & =3-K{e}^{0} \\\\ 1.5 & =3-K \\\\ K & =1.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nX(t)=3-1.5{e}^{-0.4t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the volume is $2\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X({t}_{1}) & =2 \\\\ 3-1.5{e}^{-0.4{t}_{1}} & =2 \\\\ 1 & =1.5{e}^{-0.4{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-0.4{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -0.4{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{0.4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =1.013… \\\\  & ≈1\\,\\text{minute},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}]}
